{#-
  Playbook driving protocol: render the composed procedure, walk the step table,
  resolve delegation, stop on failure, enforce review gates. Skill-agnostic —
  included by any skill that drives a playbook. Requires `context`.
-#}
## Execute

Render the composed procedure: `booping render-playbook <name>` (`<name>` = the selected playbook). Output is: notices → preamble → `## Playbook Steps` (a step table: step, its dependencies, summary, review gate) → one `## Step: <Title>` section per step, plus a `## Subgraph: <name>` intro before each subgraph's steps.

- Any `**STOP — tell the user:**` line → relay it verbatim and do not execute the playbook. `**Note — tell the user:**` → relay it, then continue.
- Follow the preamble as a playbook-level instruction over the whole run. A playbook's preamble may define resume rules for re-entering a procedure mid-run.
- Need more than a section's metadata to drive the run — e.g. the user's ask matches the playbook only partially or in a non-standard way → run `booping render-playbook <name> --step <step>` and read that step yourself. Orientation only: spawning still hands over the bootstrap prompt, never a pasted body.
- A step section may carry its body embedded in place of the fetch line (playbooks with `inline_steps: true`). Execute the embedded body directly and never run `--step` for that section — its step-scoped lessons are already appended. Detached members keep the fetch line and the bootstrap flow unchanged.
- A `## Lessons` section in the render binds you for the whole run. Step-scoped lessons reach a step through its own `--step` fetch — never copy lessons into a bootstrap prompt.
- A `## State` section in the render → the playbook is stateful; apply **Run state** below before and during the walk. No `## State` section → run state is in-conversation only.

Walk the step table top-down, running each step once every step in its `Dependencies` cell is done. Steps whose dependencies are all satisfied form a batch that runs together. For each batch:

1. Never fetch a delegated member's body. Compose a bootstrap prompt per member from its `## Step: <Title>` section alone:
   ```
   Run: booping render-playbook <name> --step <step>
   Treat its stdout as your full instruction and follow it.

   ## Run-time context
   project: {{ context.project.name if context.project else "{project}" }}
   specs_dir: {{ context.project.directory if context.project else "{vault}" }}/specs
   workdir: <workdir>
   instance: <slug>

   ## Inputs
   - <what>: <resolved value, path, or receipt>

   ## Return
   artifacts written + outcome, ≤ 5 lines
   ```
   - `workdir:` only when the run has one (see **Run state**); `instance:` only for subgraph instances.
   - `## Inputs`: the run-time values this member needs — the user's ask, artifact paths, and the receipts of the steps in its `Dependencies` cell. Nothing to pass → omit the block.
   - `## Return`: always `artifacts written + outcome, ≤ 5 lines`. The step's own body owns any richer contract.
2. Run each member per its section's detached bullet (rendered from `detached:`): model+effort → `subagent_type="general-purpose"` with that model and reasoning effort; named → `subagent_type="<name>"`; no detached bullet → you perform the step: execute the section's embedded body when it carries one, otherwise run that member's `booping render-playbook <name> --step <step>` yourself and execute its stdout as your instruction — either way under the same run-time context, inputs, and return contract. Assisted: such a body may direct you to delegate its heavy reads to `{{ config.research_agent }}` under the return contract it states — the step stays yours, only that read work leaves your context.
3. A batch of two or more steps spawns ALL their sub-agents in ONE message and never runs any of them inline; wait for all to finish before the next batch.
4. Any member failure → report it and stop; do not start the next batch.
5. After the batch completes, honor each member's `Review gate:` bullet: present that member's returned receipt labeled by step name alongside the gate text — read the artifacts it names only when the presentation needs them — and continue only on explicit user confirmation, collected via `AskUserQuestion` (confirm / request-changes options per the gate's own wording; free text arrives as "Other"). In-conversation only — no state is persisted.

Pass each step's output through unchanged to the user and into later sections that depend on it.

A step return carrying questions for the user (a `## Questions:` block or equivalent) → put the ones with enumerable options via `AskUserQuestion`, batching up to 4 per call — one entry per question, the step's stated options as the options, free text via "Other". An open-ended question — one inviting discussion or a free-form answer — stays plain chat prose; never force it into the tool. Present the rest of the return as normal output first.

### Agent ids

Record the id of every agent you spawn — delegated step or delegated reads alike — in the run artifact's `agents:` frontmatter mapping, keyed by step name. Looping back to a step within this conversation → resume its agent by that id (SendMessage) instead of spawning a fresh one, so its context carries over.

Resume only an id **this conversation recorded from its own spawn**. An id read back from the artifact on a resumed run is stale by definition: spawn fresh and overwrite the entry.

### Subgraph members

A table row tagged `*(subgraph)*` covers its `## Subgraph: <name>` intro and the inner step rows tagged `*(in <name>)*`. Expand it in place of that row:

1. Enumerate instances from the intro's `Repeat:` prose read against the output of the steps it lists under `After:` — no `Repeat:` bullet means exactly one instance. Name the enumerated instances before running.
2. Per instance, walk the inner steps in dependency order, applying protocol steps 1–5 to each batch of them, with `instance: <slug>` added to the run-time context. Every instance runs its own `--step` fetch — never fetch an inner step's body to reuse across instances. Inputs, return contracts, and `Review gate:` bullets bind per instance.
3. Run instances in parallel only when every inner step has a detached bullet; otherwise run them one at a time. Spawn at most 4 instances per message and wait for that batch before the next.
4. Report completion per instance. Any instance failure → report it naming the instance, and stop; do not start the next batch.

### Run state

Only for a render carrying a `## State` section. Every command below takes `--workdir <workdir>`.

1. Workdir: the convention the preamble names, when it names one; otherwise `{{ context.project.directory if context.project else "{vault}" }}/_runs/<playbook>/<run-slug>/`. `<run-slug>` = `{{ "{YYYYMMDD}" }}-<kebab-topic>`, date taken from the local clock — run `date +%Y%m%d`, never from memory. Create it before the first step; reuse the existing one when resuming.
2. On entry — first run and every resume — run `booping playbook-state <name> --workdir <workdir>` and start from the reported frontier: skip work whose status is already past it, re-enter at the first non-terminal status, and treat `not-started` as "bootstrap on the first transition".
3. When a step outcome matches a `When` cell of the `## State` table, judge that row's `Gates` against the artifact. Gates hold → run that state entry's `Advance:` invocation with `<to>` = the row's `To` cell (plus `--instance <slug>` for a per-instance state). Gates fail → do not transition; report what is missing and stop.
4. The transition report is authoritative — relay it and move on. Never re-read the artifact to verify a transition.
5. Never hand-edit an artifact's `status:`, `confirmed:`, or any other hook-written frontmatter key — including in a workdir file a `frontmatter-update <file> ...` hook targets. `booping playbook-transition` is the only writer of run state.
6. A non-zero exit from `booping playbook-transition` → relay its stderr verbatim and stop.
